# Research integrity

The goal is a true answer, recorded so a thesis examiner can trace it. A strategy that beats buy-and-hold because it
was tuned until it did is a failure of this project.

## Never

1. **The holdout.** Never read, plot or summarize the registered holdout window or anything after its start
   (`so.core.holdout`, `records/holdout_windows.csv`), never read the quarantine, never set `SO_QUARANTINE_PATH`,
   `SO_HOLDOUT_PATH` or `SO_HOLDOUT_EVALUATION_TOKEN`, never run `scripts/holdout_window.py`,
   `scripts/holdout_evaluate.py` or `scripts/refresh_development_view.py`. Only Nicolas does. Tests may set those
   variables to temporary folders and fake values, on synthetic data only.
2. **Data outside the point-in-time layer.** A design reads data only through the runner's view
   (`so.core.point_in_time`, `docs/DATA_SOURCES.md`). Never use a value whose `available_ts` is after the decision;
   a model trained at time t uses only labels whose `label_known_ts` is before t. No new data source without Nicolas
   (write the proposal). The VIX files in `store01_rawzone/ibkr_vix_family/` are approved, read-only.
3. **Protected files** (`protected_paths.txt`): no change. Existing values in `so/config.py` never change; new constants
   with tests are proposed to Nicolas.
4. **Data files.** Never modify or delete anything in `store01`-`store03`. In `store04_experiments/` add only; the
   trial log is appended only through `so.core.trial_log`. Never change file permissions or run as administrator.
5. **Results.** Never delete or rewrite a past result; add a dated correction. Never re-run to "see if it changes",
   pick the best of several runs, drop a candidate or a period after seeing results, or change success criteria or
   baselines after seeing results. A result that beats buy-and-hold is a **candidate pending review**, not a success.
6. **Packages and git.** Never install, upgrade or remove a package. Never rewrite history, force-push or delete
   branches.

## Pre-registration

Before the first run of a new experiment or version, commit `PROTOCOL.md` (question, hypothesis, success criteria,
data, rules, the complete candidate list, selection rule, baselines including the uninformed version of each informed
component, stopping rule, limitations, trial budget), `config.py`, `design.py` and tests, with a message starting
`preregister expNN`. A change after a run is a new `PROTOCOL_VERSION` with the reason in the protocol's change log,
labelled data-dependent if results motivated it. At most two new versions per experiment. Keep grids small (about 3-15
candidates). When discussing significance, give the number of trials in the trial log behind the conclusion.
